#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 960.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222332} :activity/id #uuid "bf2387fe-ebed-5568-a260-0d681cb9fd28" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050228 :topic/id #uuid "37d183d8-ce59-581c-9a89-fec7544aae03" :topic/math-academy-id 6376} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222332 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222502 :tutorial/content "Consider the homogeneous linear system\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}' = A\\mathbf{x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an $n\\times n$ matrix.\n\nA **fundamental matrix** for this system is an $n\\times n$ matrix $\\Phi(t)$ whose columns are $n$ linearly independent solutions of the system.\n\nIf $\\mathbf{x}_1(t),\\dots,\\mathbf{x}_n(t)$ are linearly independent solutions, then one fundamental matrix is\n\n$$\n\\Phi(t)=\\big[\\mathbf{x}_1(t),\\dots,\\mathbf{x}_n(t)\\big].\n$$\n\n**Note:** The columns of $\\Phi(t)$ can be written in any order.\n\nLet's see an example." :tutorial/id #uuid "a7207a24-194d-59d6-b25c-f9bb4f1b0d6e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14508 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "3b0bd285-b383-5ebb-821f-4f74a0b37719" :step/math-academy-id 594316 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222333}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222333 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221420 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221191 :question/id #uuid "4874a486-2cf1-5b6e-ae4f-52579f03b077" :question/math-academy-id "e-21695"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "af871f3d-da9e-5fd3-a61f-94e155ccf2b3" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049851}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221985} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221999} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308599} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308600} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308601} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398781} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442556} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442557} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442562}] :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Fundamental Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "aa3c7790-3ab9-5519-bad3-4f82fc8ac113" :step/math-academy-id 594317 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222334}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222334 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222503 :tutorial/content "Again, let $\\Phi(t)$ be a fundamental matrix for the linear system\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}' = A\\mathbf{x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an $n\\times n$ matrix. The fundamental matrices have the following important properties.\n\n1\\. *Satisfies the underlying system*. Indeed, since each column of $\\Phi(t)$ is a solution of the system, we have\n\n  $$\n  \\Phi'(t) = A\\,\\Phi(t).\n  $$\n\n2\\. *Invertibility*. The columns of $\\Phi(t)$ are linearly independent for all $t,$ so\n\n  $$\n  \\det\\Phi(t)\\neq 0\n  $$\n\n  for all $t.$ In particular, $\\Phi(t)$ is invertible for all $t.$\n\n3\\. *Representation of solutions*. Every solution $\\mathbf{x}(t)$ of the system can be written in the form\n\n  $$\n  \\mathbf{x}(t)=\\Phi(t)\\,\\mathbf{c},\n  $$\n\n  where $\\mathbf{c}$ is a constant vector.\n\n4\\. *Non-uniqueness*. If $C$ is any invertible constant matrix, then\n\n  $$\n  \\Psi(t)=\\Phi(t)\\,C\n  $$\n\n  is also a fundamental matrix for the same system.\n\nWe'll now get some practice." :tutorial/id #uuid "e2fdbc29-4542-5fdc-adbd-316c5dcb6878" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14509 :tutorial/title "Properties of the Fundamental Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "2fe7252e-7fc7-59bb-bc32-74c452b7cf73" :step/math-academy-id 594320 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222335}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222335 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221421 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221193 :question/id #uuid "a52a6539-60d1-557c-a042-cbce5616e94f" :question/math-academy-id "e-21697"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0aea7503-74d6-5ba4-9411-ee53e59512d0" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221990} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221991} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311648} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311687} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365731} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391264} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396895} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396916} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442561} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498522} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498584} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186533984}] :knowledge-point/title "Proving Properties of a Fundamental Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "2b90cd12-9695-5b6b-b407-6eed6ae7fc84" :step/math-academy-id 594321 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222336}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222336 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222504 :tutorial/content "For a linear system $\\mathbf{x}' = A\\mathbf{x}$ with constant $n\\times n$ matrix $A,$ the **standard fundamental matrix** is the unique fundamental matrix $\\Phi_0(t)$ that satisfies the initial condition\n\n$$\n\\Phi_0(0)=I_n,\n$$\n\nwhere $I_n$ is the $n \\times n$ identity matrix.\n\nFor constant matrices $A,$ the standard fundamental matrix is given by\n\n$$\n\\Phi_0(t)=e^{At}.\n$$\n\nThis matrix satisfies\n\n$$\n\\Phi_0'(t)=A\\,\\Phi_0(t)\n\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad\n\\Phi_0(0)=I_n.\n$$\n\n**Note:** Any other fundamental matrix $\\Phi(t)$ for the same system can be written in the form\n\n$$\n\\Phi(t)=\\Phi_0(t)\\,C,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an invertible constant matrix.\n\nIn the next slide, we will compute the standard fundamental matrix for a specific example." :tutorial/id #uuid "4f75576c-be32-5784-9fee-e1288445728c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14510 :tutorial/title "Fundamental Matrices and Matrix Exponentials"} :step/id #uuid "d44c84d5-4215-5318-bba2-81eb3a34ee7d" :step/math-academy-id 594325 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222337}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222337 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221422 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221192 :question/id #uuid "3e053d6d-f051-5c4c-a20b-1e90b2d5941b" :question/math-academy-id "e-21696"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "66c99cda-25a5-50f2-903b-bfb1fdc5fa10" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050054}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221988} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221989} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308497} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308609} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308611} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308634} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365659} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365668} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365673} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442558} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442559} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442560} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498520} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534093}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing Fundamental Matrices Using Matrix Exponentials"} :step/id #uuid "32e0e465-9256-5f88-b771-2edff24ecd54" :step/math-academy-id 594326}] :activity/title "Fundamental Matrices" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186221468}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498623} :activity/id #uuid "cfe34345-755d-5da2-bb57-2997badcd43f" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050228 :topic/id #uuid "37d183d8-ce59-581c-9a89-fec7544aae03" :topic/math-academy-id 6376} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498607 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311648 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1666e2ac-b3fe-5abf-bf6c-480c904e35c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "properties of invertible matrices" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308916} {:answer/id #uuid "2a0e242e-f8bc-5b61-92f4-057c108c04c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "definition of matrix multiplication" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309064} {:answer/id #uuid "39e9ae43-ec84-53c1-9ff5-1cab8a1513fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "substituting ${Φ}^{′}=A\\,Φ$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309193} {:answer/id #uuid "88c4bae8-f281-5b85-b676-befbbaf6a7b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "definition of a fundamental matrix" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309823}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308916} :answer-field/id #uuid "2dd0c097-ae2f-556e-9141-4922553f97a2" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310913} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d589487-f539-5a2a-9c68-978fcbd8d916" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Φ(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308974} {:answer/id #uuid "4f7f3a02-4a57-5a6f-82ac-295a09b04201" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309351} {:answer/id #uuid "801400d7-5f92-5160-aa74-65afe20e1479" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{A}^{-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309754} {:answer/id #uuid "f6c6ca05-bdfb-57f3-acf6-42e584e9e189" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{Φ}^{-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310710}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308974} :answer-field/id #uuid "60ac3f93-6295-5f30-bd13-d2e6c1a521c6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311049} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5b75abdf-0c2f-5971-941e-b26da9a01df8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "properties of invertible matrices" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309435} {:answer/id #uuid "85a84435-5c8a-5585-b8e5-e8bffe321073" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "substituting ${Φ}^{′}=A\\,Φ$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309787} {:answer/id #uuid "eac98a1a-0bc6-5585-badc-67bd9c21bfee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "definition of a fundamental matrix" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310617} {:answer/id #uuid "fe87b04f-2863-5764-85be-e1341c2b05ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "definition of matrix multiplication" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310783}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310617} :answer-field/id #uuid "b40a4c73-e790-56ac-96c9-d5fa994ccaf7" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311314}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3d9725c1-fcc5-57a9-8b5a-bd5efbda8207" :question/math-academy-id "q-336093" :question/problem "Consider the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant $2\\times 2$ matrix. Let $Φ(t)$ be a fundamental matrix for this system. A derivation showing that $Φ(t)$ is invertible for all $t$ is given below.\n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ Let ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ be the columns of $Φ(t)$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are linearly independent for all $t$\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ $det(Φ(t))\\ne 0$ for all $t$\n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ $Φ(t)$ is invertible for all $t$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L2}$ states the independence of the columns of {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L2}$ follows from $\\text{L1}$ by the {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L4}$ follows from line $\\text{L3}$ by the {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's justify each of the selected steps.\n\n- We first interpret line $\\text{L2}.$ Line $\\text{L2}$ states that the two columns of $Φ(t)$ are linearly independent, which is required of a fundamental matrix. Thus, $\\text{L2}$ states independence of the columns of $Φ(t).$\n\n- Next, we consider lines $\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L2}.$ Since $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix, its columns ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ form a basis of the solution space for our system. Thus, $\\text{L2}$ follows from $\\text{L1}$ by the $\\text{definition of a fundamental matrix}.$\n\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\text{L3}$ and $\\text{L4}.$ Recall that a square matrix is invertible when its determinant is nonzero. Since in $\\text{L3}$ we have that $det(Φ(t))\\ne 0$ for all $t,$ it follows that $Φ(t)$ is invertible for all $t.$ Thus, $\\text{L4}$ follows from $\\text{L3}$ by the $\\text{properties of invertible matrices}.$"} :step/id #uuid "0d32e49b-f44f-5aaf-b893-9c5777c8ec8a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498653}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498619 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498522 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09d0162a-0c31-57bd-bb88-fdfa4dc8e68e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "columns" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497822} {:answer/id #uuid "5f91266e-571d-5556-9e28-0c64ca72bbde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "diagonals" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498025} {:answer/id #uuid "ce4ccd02-457b-505d-be66-2a3ce1983dc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "first row" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498265} {:answer/id #uuid "e0717cf0-2277-5799-aeb4-bd7df48e84fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "rows" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498301}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497822} :answer-field/id #uuid "691f6c70-3e94-5cde-bb06-839111864eb1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498407} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4909c353-7e8a-5cb8-b25e-ad22a33b46c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "properties of diagonalizable matrices" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497966} {:answer/id #uuid "4a9b9084-c9c1-58b5-a890-2458f7916ba6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "definition of matrix multiplication" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497970} {:answer/id #uuid "aca1d897-83fa-5953-b387-6aa8cac56164" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "definition of a fundamental matrix" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498183} {:answer/id #uuid "d0d5cf45-87b2-58ae-b6b6-07a8c858f7b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "properties of invertible matrices" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498272}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498183} :answer-field/id #uuid "78b2f140-f551-5793-a29c-4fb476575aec" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498419} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37508ceb-d284-5a06-b3ec-936f412805af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{A}^{-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497922} {:answer/id #uuid "674daa87-495d-58ab-9af5-9cdc5062e82d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Φ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498045} {:answer/id #uuid "7d9c66a3-67a6-5fd0-9447-e4938b51f47e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{Φ}^{-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498094} {:answer/id #uuid "a2a29fc1-da0c-59ab-8e24-af6b4d9da47a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498161}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498045} :answer-field/id #uuid "848fedd1-7778-5bd5-81c5-da60ff5ead33" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498425} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ef6c5e9-0b48-5bca-8fc3-2d7acdfe98a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{L2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497988} {:answer/id #uuid "726a5dc3-65f5-538d-af5b-3ba8daedca86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{L3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498071} {:answer/id #uuid "83149379-a893-5cb3-ad7b-b43718777042" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{L4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498104} {:answer/id #uuid "e0da1ffb-345f-5ae1-91cb-7693db413e1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{L1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498302}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498071} :answer-field/id #uuid "86e5a0f2-8f10-5531-ae7d-52d463866902" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498426}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3fdf52f0-09f2-54b2-864b-627c885837ad" :question/math-academy-id "q-336086" :question/problem "Consider the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant $2\\times 2$ matrix. Let $Φ(t)$ be a fundamental matrix for this system. A derivation showing that $Φ(t)$ is invertible for all $t$ is given below.\n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ Let ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ be the columns of $Φ(t)$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are linearly independent for all $t$\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ $det(Φ(t))\\ne 0$ for all $t$\n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ $Φ(t)$ is invertible for all $t$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L1}$ introduces the notation for the {{field-1}} of {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L2}$ follows from $\\text{L1}$ by the {{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L4}$ follows from line {{field-4}} by the properties of invertible matrices." :question/worked-solution "Let's justify each of the selected steps.\n\n- We first interpret line $\\text{L1}.$ Line $\\text{L1}$ introduces the notation for the $\\text{columns}$ of $Φ,$ namely the functions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$ These functions are the fundamental solutions of the system.\n\n- Next, we consider lines $\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L2}.$ Since $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix, its columns ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ form a basis of the solution space for our system. Thus, $\\text{L2}$ follows from $\\text{L1}$ by the $\\text{definition of a fundamental matrix}.$\n\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\text{L3}$ and $\\text{L4}.$ Recall that a square matrix is invertible when its determinant is nonzero. Since in $\\text{L3}$ we have that $det(Φ(t))\\ne 0$ for all $t,$ it follows that $Φ(t)$ is invertible for all $t.$ Thus, $\\text{L4}$ follows from $\\text{L3}$ by the properties of invertible matrices."} :step/id #uuid "297b5ab8-1639-57a8-8dcf-ef5dd67ae845" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498607}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498620 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498520 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dae93f70-eae4-559e-a461-fdfe0abd280a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "e^{7t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498287}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498287} :answer-field/id #uuid "3daf4d3b-396f-535d-ab9e-524aea10fa43" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498387} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8a35fdcb-73b8-57ed-b501-fbab000af654" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498117}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498117} :answer-field/id #uuid "766ab29d-e5ba-5a2d-8e7a-62126c5689d2" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498415} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ef4cb3a8-c477-5885-9483-f1d0949fce53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498323}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498323} :answer-field/id #uuid "913d9aeb-35ba-5403-99b5-0421203894a3" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498431} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34c83096-dcf8-5094-a182-51ebd39feed2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "e^{4t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497916}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497916} :answer-field/id #uuid "b3ef4a65-1363-5281-b2e0-253a6eed11ad" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498448}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3f77715c-0903-5f4b-a449-6816c6d9bb06" :question/math-academy-id "q-334465" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear ODEs ${x}^{′}=Ax$ with the matrix $A$ shown above. Compute the **standard** fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ for this system.\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">${Φ}_{0}(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $A$ is a constant $n\\times n$ matrix and $x(t)$ is an $n\\times 1$ column vector. The standard fundamental matrix of the linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ denoted ${Φ}_{0}(t),$ is the unique matrix solution of the initial value problem \n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}^{′}(t)=A{Φ}_{0}(t),\\,{Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere ${I}_{n}$ is the identity matrix. \n\nFurthermore, it can be shown that ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}.$\n\nWe have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7\\end{bmatrix}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are constants to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The eigenvalues of $At$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=4t\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=7t\n$$\n\neach of multiplicity one. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}r(4t) &={e}^{4t} \\\\ r(7t) &={e}^{7t}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0} &={e}^{4t} \\\\ 7t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0} &={e}^{7t}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n- Subtract the first equation from the second one, we get\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}=\\frac{{e}^{7t}-{e}^{4t}}{3t}.\n$$\n\n- Substituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n4t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{4t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{0}=\\frac{7{e}^{4t}-4{e}^{7t}}{3}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the diagonal entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =4\\cdot \\frac{{e}^{7t}-{e}^{4t}}{3t}\\cdot t+\\frac{7{e}^{4t}-4{e}^{7t}}{3} \\\\  & ={e}^{4t} \\\\ 7{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =7\\cdot \\frac{{e}^{7t}-{e}^{4t}}{3t}\\cdot t+\\frac{7{e}^{4t}-4{e}^{7t}}{3} \\\\  & ={e}^{7t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{4t} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {e}^{7t}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "3ad30acf-66b9-5977-ad36-2183bb8c58c8" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498619}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498623 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365659 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e09b9bd8-e653-5eb4-99a5-e24ca1275327" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}(6t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365661} {:answer/id #uuid "06aa85f1-3426-574e-99c1-322400e91930" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sin(6t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497814}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497814} :answer-field/id #uuid "4302394f-a0de-5281-a556-4c43f2010e3e" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365660} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "809ccb92-46bf-593f-aa3e-fab259413c63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}(6t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365663} {:answer/id #uuid "4148c3f6-7cd3-5b8a-8b0b-2b61c22f4d78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\cos(6t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497945}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497945} :answer-field/id #uuid "3baf4fff-2224-5b2e-97a5-8950130f9bc3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365662} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17f33320-09c2-5afb-8ad2-f98194eb8e4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}(6t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365665} {:answer/id #uuid "0d0d2d29-ff61-5f5f-b6b0-49c8e2ecf0ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\sin(6t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497827}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497827} :answer-field/id #uuid "17ad3991-2ac6-5ad3-a913-929c3b64178c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365664} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ac97a07e-cd49-5c84-a334-f6d1792e26ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}(6t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365667} {:answer/id #uuid "873e1c15-5d9d-58fe-a62d-ab1be804a1ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\cos(6t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498111}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498111} :answer-field/id #uuid "918694ef-b209-599f-9859-2c7968e720c9" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365666}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "511649c4-cd9c-5496-8df1-bb8892a20547" :question/math-academy-id "q-334386" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -6 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear ODEs ${x}^{′}=Ax$ with the matrix $A$ shown above. Compute the **standard** fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ for this system.\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">${Φ}_{0}(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr></tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $A$ is a constant $n\\times n$ matrix and $x(t)$ is an $n\\times 1$ column vector. The standard fundamental matrix of the linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ denoted ${Φ}_{0}(t),$ is the unique matrix solution of the initial value problem \n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}^{′}(t)=A{Φ}_{0}(t),\\,{Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere ${I}_{n}$ is the identity matrix. \n\nFurthermore, it can be shown that ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}.$\n\nWe have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -6 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{bmatrix}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{\\alpha }_{0} & -6{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ 6{\\alpha }_{1}t & {\\alpha }_{0}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are constants to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The eigenvalues of $At$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=6\\text{i}t,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-6\\text{i}t,\n$$\n\nwhich are complex conjugates. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r((6\\text{i})t) & ={e}^{(6\\text{i})t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}(6\\text{i})t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{0}+6\\text{i}{\\alpha }_{1}t & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we equate the real and imaginary parts, and solve for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0} &=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t) \\\\ 6{\\alpha }_{1}t &=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0} &=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1} &=\\frac{1}{6t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t) \\\\ 6{\\alpha }_{1}t & =6\\cdot \\frac{1}{6t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)\\cdot t \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "3ea51f6a-6ea8-5162-ad6a-900689393007" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498620}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498653 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498584 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3bd46542-9a80-5921-aec9-c4e0eab15d02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\text{L2}$ and $\\text{L4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497935} {:answer/id #uuid "4a0c74f4-d2e2-5a08-a0ec-15c44fc1a4f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\text{L3}$ and $\\text{L4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497968} {:answer/id #uuid "9789113b-b31f-54b4-b8fe-4662096868cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498144} {:answer/id #uuid "d57a43c8-dd84-5c2d-96ed-8415ae761576" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498277}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497968} :answer-field/id #uuid "3b347ff5-a360-5553-944a-1fd66d4ece0a" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498386} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "64196c4e-29d7-5962-b257-222a15c4588d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "the definition of a basis" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498039} {:answer/id #uuid "ce4515ff-f000-5d07-9351-f9cf05460b2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "definition of matrix multiplication" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498264} {:answer/id #uuid "cf0b0954-c00e-51da-a8ca-5afe64dfb0ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "integrating the given system" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498269} {:answer/id #uuid "efe24b44-977f-576a-b20f-de491d1bb43f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "multiplying $Φ(t)$ with its inverse" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498325}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498264} :answer-field/id #uuid "69513af4-694f-5ca8-996c-0c33d8fa859d" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498408} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c1b43a8-0bad-584d-8658-946b10a53deb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "definition of matrix multiplication" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497825} {:answer/id #uuid "f03bcfca-1c79-5dcc-a769-287b903a87de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "the definition of a basis" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498327} {:answer/id #uuid "fd2fe107-c83d-541c-ad1f-d9f4feb898de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "multiplying $Φ(t)$ with its inverse" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498353} {:answer/id #uuid "fd8a0c7f-af95-54f9-89f1-fb7222915fbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "integrating the given system" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498356}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498327} :answer-field/id #uuid "84273087-2e18-501d-872d-5ad466c8d262" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498424}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d656b9e3-27c9-51a6-a70f-c9d56643be83" :question/math-academy-id "q-336108" :question/problem "Consider the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant $2\\times 2$ matrix. Let $Φ(t)$ be a fundamental matrix for this system. A derivation showing that every solution $x(t)$ can be written in the form $x(t)=Φ(t)\\,c$ for some constant vector $c=[{c}_{1},\\,{c}_{2}]^{T}$ is given below.\n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ Let ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ be the columns of $Φ(t)$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ form a basis of solutions\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ $x(t)$ can be written as $x(t)={c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t)$\n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ $Φ(t)\\,c={c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t)$\n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ $x(t)=Φ(t)\\,c$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L3}$ follows from line $\\text{L2}$ by {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L4}$ follows from line $\\text{L1}$ by the {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L5}$ follows from lines {{field-3}} by the transitive property of equality." :question/worked-solution "Let's justify each of the selected steps.\n\n- We first consider lines $\\text{L2}$ and $\\text{L3}.$ Once ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ form a basis, every solution $x(t)$ of the system can be expressed uniquely as a linear combination\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{L3}$ follows from $\\text{L2}$ by $\\text{the definition of a basis}.$\n\n- Next, we consider lines $\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L4}.$ Since ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are the columns of $Φ(t),$ we can write\n\n$$\nΦ(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\,| & | \\\\ {x}_{1}(t) & {x}_{2}(t) \\\\ \\,| & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThen by the definition of matrix multiplication,\n\n$$\nΦ(t)\\,c=\\begin{bmatrix}\\,| & | \\\\ {x}_{1}(t) & {x}_{2}(t) \\\\ \\,| & |\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}={c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t).\n$$\n\nThus, $\\text{L4}$ follows from $\\text{L1}$ by the $\\text{definition of matrix multiplication}.$\n\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\text{L3}$ and $\\text{L4}.$ In $\\text{L3}$ we have $x(t)={c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t),$ and in $\\text{L4}$ we have $Φ(t)c={c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t).$ Since both expressions are equal to the same quantity, the transitive property of equality gives $x(t)=Φ(t)c.$ Thus, $\\text{L5}$ follows from $\\text{L3 and L4}.$"} :step/id #uuid "db020f43-44ad-58e9-afc7-d835ef2b9210"}] :activity/title "Fundamental Matrices" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497795}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046645} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534127} :activity/id #uuid "72200359-34e6-5d5b-9196-498c2e2739b8" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050228 :topic/id #uuid "37d183d8-ce59-581c-9a89-fec7544aae03" :topic/math-academy-id 6376} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534109 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186533984 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21f04a48-e3d9-5285-9be6-a47bfb7a3162" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{L4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533221} {:answer/id #uuid "806ffe8b-ec3d-5752-b9c6-7e479c231b96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{L2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533443} {:answer/id #uuid "bf7ddf96-482e-5261-9f56-9cabb5f0cd3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{L1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533600} {:answer/id #uuid "d4ac113d-a092-55a0-828c-3cce578364b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{L3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533658}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186533443} :answer-field/id #uuid "047d2299-ba3b-5217-bd76-392b196edadf" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533773} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "175e98b0-2179-5c38-aec4-02ea135fddfa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{Φ}^{-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533192} {:answer/id #uuid "4fb78a1e-acbc-582f-9896-4a25b60643c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Φ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533330} {:answer/id #uuid "85fcf6d7-1b0b-5bf0-afee-87974db835f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533455} {:answer/id #uuid "ce4797d8-fe35-5b5b-b08e-1c83d6082952" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{A}^{-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533639}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186533330} :answer-field/id #uuid "20fff794-00dd-50a8-9256-5d3d679c4c80" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533806} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fa5012c-8bff-5182-bdc1-c3192d2fa42b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "properties of diagonalizable matrices" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533179} {:answer/id #uuid "c9a4e473-005c-5bad-b050-c349b4619ab7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "integrating the given system" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533622} {:answer/id #uuid "cfe5eb6f-4d7a-5247-87cc-a29690e1564d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "definition of matrix multiplication" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533644} {:answer/id #uuid "e959566f-e723-58b3-9ab2-a51a923ed3cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "definition of a fundamental matrix" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533714}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186533714} :answer-field/id #uuid "99e500e9-2419-5436-86f7-bd0983f12961" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533896} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a59e584-278d-59db-b6ea-7b7977de0f71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "columns" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533159} {:answer/id #uuid "79c8042c-d474-5943-aed4-1c689601cb7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "diagonals" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533428} {:answer/id #uuid "b483bb15-790a-5e86-a64e-5ba317eecac7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "first row" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533578} {:answer/id #uuid "cc4405cf-0772-5f28-9f99-4a05e4a8fb85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "rows" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533632}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186533159} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8b3efd3-b7c3-58e8-b4b9-9e7263c863ee" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533964}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "20281150-3bf1-5302-a5be-765695a3b8d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-336094" :question/problem "Consider the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant $2\\times 2$ matrix. Let $Φ(t)$ be a fundamental matrix for this system. A derivation showing that $Φ(t)$ is invertible for all $t$ is given below.\n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ Let ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ be the columns of $Φ(t)$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are linearly independent for all $t$\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ $det(Φ(t))\\ne 0$ for all $t$\n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ $Φ(t)$ is invertible for all $t$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L1}$ introduces the notation for the {{field-1}} of {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L2}$ follows from $\\text{L1}$ by the {{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L3}$ follows from line {{field-4}} by the properties of determinants." :question/worked-solution "Let's justify each of the selected steps.\n\n- We first interpret line $\\text{L1}.$ Line $\\text{L1}$ introduces the notation for the $\\text{columns}$ of $Φ,$ namely the functions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$ These functions are the fundamental solutions of the system.\n\n- Next, we consider lines $\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L2}.$ Since $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix, its columns ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ form a basis of the solution space for our system. Thus, $\\text{L2}$ follows from $\\text{L1}$ by the $\\text{definition of a fundamental matrix}.$\n\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\text{L2}$ and $\\text{L3}.$ Recall that a matrix has linearly independent columns if and only if its determinant is nonzero. Since ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are linearly independent for any particular $t,$\n\n$$\ndet(Φ(t))\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{L3}$ follows from line $\\text{L2}$ by the properties of determinants."} :step/id #uuid "231eb329-513b-5dfb-85da-6e0f13afe8fd"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534111 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398781 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7c007ca0-acef-5af6-b3fe-264373817071" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2{e}^{-1} & 2{e}^{-1} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-1}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398783} {:answer/id #uuid "59ce7ec4-615c-508f-9c8e-4b5a5d18d797" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-t} & 0 \\\\ 2t{e}^{-t} & 2{e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398784} {:answer/id #uuid "b333daa7-d3c9-5885-83dd-c52477a27945" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2t \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398785} {:answer/id #uuid "1281b7c7-c44e-5d28-ae85-689e0d3ae235" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2{e}^{-t} & 2t{e}^{-t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398786} {:answer/id #uuid "de8dcb0a-6338-525e-8932-5efde71571f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398787}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398786} :answer-field/id #uuid "f91e0298-1d33-5125-a1ef-22a1c3b27757" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398782}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6ce970ec-3f9d-57d6-9307-1253ccd1b689" :question/math-academy-id "q-334326" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}x,\\,{x}_{1}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t},\\,{x}_{2}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2t \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear ODEs and two of its linearly independent solutions ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2},$ shown above. Which of the following is a fundamental matrix for the system?" :question/worked-solution "Given a linear system ${x}^{′}=Ax,$ where $A$ is an $n\\times n$ matrix, a *fundamental matrix* $Φ(t)$ of the system is an $n\\times n$ matrix whose columns are linearly independent solutions of the system.\n\nIn our case, we have a $2\\times 2$ system. We are also given that the two linearly independent solutions of the system are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}=\\begin{bmatrix}2{e}^{-t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {x}_{2}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}2t \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}=\\begin{bmatrix}2t{e}^{-t} \\\\ {e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, our fundamental could be written as\n\n$$\nΦ(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2{e}^{-t} & 2t{e}^{-t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Note:** The columns of $Φ$ can be written in any order."} :step/id #uuid "24523b4e-4e57-53cd-8b40-7f56c7c2589e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534142}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534127 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221991 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b918900-9d47-501a-8fe9-03921f6ed92d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "properties of invertible matrices" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305722} {:answer/id #uuid "5dbdc32e-065c-5332-9d8e-eb72d3130e00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "definition of a fundamental matrix" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306051} {:answer/id #uuid "6b45a51c-d763-5bfb-9f79-8f1fadd5a426" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "definition of a fundamental matrix" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306178} {:answer/id #uuid "6d2b391a-a7cf-5a3f-b395-8b5343a7d633" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "properties of diagonalizable matrices" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306196} {:answer/id #uuid "d400de09-c2d8-5f6f-8258-d35170cbcf5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "definition of matrix multiplication" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307116}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306051} :answer-field/id #uuid "97f4577b-63be-5c98-bcf0-c658896d4179" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307999} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c29a02a-5ddf-5ffe-8562-988f1e1a9aff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "the definition of a basis" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305255} {:answer/id #uuid "316d241a-8a8a-5270-84e3-f0fc3ef40261" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "adding the vectors ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305617} {:answer/id #uuid "39f356a8-8fda-5c4b-9454-f7cb76d1ea0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "integrating the given system" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305702} {:answer/id #uuid "600321e5-9661-57d4-a3e1-64da86996880" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "multiplying $Φ(t)$ with it's inverse" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306076} {:answer/id #uuid "e313c524-01fc-5609-ae4c-14eafceafe00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "the definition of a basis" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307255}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305255} :answer-field/id #uuid "9bd31540-fe17-5fac-bdc9-cc5602d470d7" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308012} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9df8332a-0028-5851-a541-699e5fb5b445" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "definition of matrix multiplication" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306628} {:answer/id #uuid "a35b581b-96f5-5832-8aa2-3b756a1be5cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "definition of a fundamental matrix" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306689} {:answer/id #uuid "b096b37f-397b-59cd-b45a-7a82f14b6d6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "fact that $Φ$ is nonsingular" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306813} {:answer/id #uuid "edcd7607-2741-5c34-acfc-0d847398cbb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "properties of invertible matrices" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307336} {:answer/id #uuid "edd1095a-2f45-5472-aa18-be05cd67e567" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "definition of matrix multiplication" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307337}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186307337} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5be50c8-6e0b-5b31-b6e5-a6b8db5b8b63" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308245}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7179862e-a345-5508-93d4-73732015d190" :question/math-academy-id "q-333723" :question/problem "Consider the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant $2\\times 2$ matrix. Let $Φ(t)$ be a fundamental matrix for this system. A derivation showing that every solution $x(t)$ can be written in the form $x(t)=Φ(t)\\,c$ for some constant vector $c=[{c}_{1},\\,{c}_{2}]^{T}$ is given below.\n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ Let ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ be the columns of $Φ(t)$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ form a basis of solutions\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ $x(t)$ can be written as $x(t)={c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t)$\n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ $Φ(t)\\,c={c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t)$\n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ $x(t)=Φ(t)\\,c$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L2}$ follows from $\\text{L1}$ by the {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L3}$ follows from line $\\text{L2}$ by {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L4}$ follows from line $\\text{L1}$ by the {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's justify each of the selected steps.\n\n- We first consider lines $\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L2}.$ Since $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix, its columns ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ form a basis of the solution space for our system. Thus, $\\text{L2}$ follows from $\\text{L1}$ by the $\\text{definition of a fundamental matrix}.$\n\n- Next, we consider lines $\\text{L2}$ and $\\text{L3}.$ Once ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ form a basis, every solution $x(t)$ of the system can be expressed uniquely as a linear combination\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{L3}$ follows from $\\text{L2}$ by $\\text{the definition of a basis}.$\n\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L4}.$ Since ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are the columns of $Φ(t),$ we can write\n\n$$\nΦ(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\,| & | \\\\ {x}_{1}(t) & {x}_{2}(t) \\\\ \\,| & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThen by the definition of matrix multiplication,\n\n$$\nΦ(t)\\,c=\\begin{bmatrix}\\,| & | \\\\ {x}_{1}(t) & {x}_{2}(t) \\\\ \\,| & |\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}={c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t).\n$$\n\nThus, $\\text{L4}$ follows from $\\text{L1}$ by the $\\text{definition of matrix multiplication}.$"} :step/id #uuid "62363ed1-22ab-56fa-9971-8c017f526c79" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534111}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534128 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365673 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5728e179-6831-5401-a928-e641db0a06b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{-4t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365675}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365675} :answer-field/id #uuid "afbc2e57-8b1b-5ce4-9b27-cb6cab8bfe1d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365674} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "979acb26-7ddd-5b55-9211-9925729aa071" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365677}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365677} :answer-field/id #uuid "43739f8d-9cc2-5a3f-8f44-3fd681edf8db" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365676} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8029db2d-8bde-50dd-9836-f39ca854a3bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "t{e}^{-4t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365679}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365679} :answer-field/id #uuid "faaddf78-a1db-5496-a708-d82d0e79d47b" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365678} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6356927b-4df6-547a-97be-5637f834898e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{-4t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365681}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365681} :answer-field/id #uuid "c911bdd1-5a68-53a2-90c8-77d2d0783bc8" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365680}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "93c5b9c1-d3c4-52c5-af95-03ae43585aaf" :question/math-academy-id "q-334497" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear ODEs ${x}^{′}=Ax$ with the matrix $A$ shown above. Compute the **standard** fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ for this system.\n\n| ${Φ}_{0}(t)=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $A$ is a constant $n\\times n$ matrix and $x(t)$ is an $n\\times 1$ column vector. The standard fundamental matrix of the linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ denoted ${Φ}_{0}(t),$ is the unique matrix solution of the initial value problem \n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}^{′}(t)=A{Φ}_{0}(t),\\,{Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere ${I}_{n}$ is the identity matrix. \n\nFurthermore, it can be shown that ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}.$\n\nWe have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & {\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ 0 & -4{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are constants to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The only eigenvalue of $At$ is \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=-4t\n$$\n\nof multiplicity two. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}r(-4t) &={e}^{-4t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\lambda }r(-4t) &={e}^{-4t}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-4t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0} &={e}^{-4t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1} &={e}^{-4t}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n- From the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{-4t}.\n$$\n\n- Substituting this into the first one, we get\n\n$$\n-4t{e}^{-4t}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{-4t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{-4t}(1+4t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =-4{e}^{-4t}t+{e}^{-4t}(1+4t) \\\\  & ={e}^{-4t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}t & ={e}^{-4t}t \\\\  & =t{e}^{-4t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-4t} & t{e}^{-4t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-4t}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "6372c740-c40b-54b4-b2d8-d67db30bf1d4" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534109}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534142 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534093 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a54e01d-d3da-520d-a71e-fb2b3d3a8bd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "e^{6t}+e^{-6t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533275}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186533275} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ec158fd-889b-5066-b795-fdf52e06926c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533801} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f40c120d-2ef8-574c-b604-70f11f9600d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "e^{6t}+e^{-6t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533740}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186533740} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ac691a2-04d5-52ef-bc49-8d3327cd84e3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533851}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "f69ba2b0-76ef-5087-a5bf-906e0adf24a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-334388" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear ODEs ${x}^{′}=Ax$ with the matrix $A$ shown above. Compute the **standard** fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ for this system.\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">${Φ}_{0}(t)=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"80px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"80px\">${e}^{6t}-{e}^{-6t}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"80px\">${e}^{6t}-{e}^{-6t}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"80px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr></tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $A$ is a constant $n\\times n$ matrix and $x(t)$ is an $n\\times 1$ column vector. The standard fundamental matrix of the linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ denoted ${Φ}_{0}(t),$ is the unique matrix solution of the initial value problem \n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}^{′}(t)=A{Φ}_{0}(t),\\,{Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere ${I}_{n}$ is the identity matrix. \n\nFurthermore, it can be shown that ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}.$\n\nWe have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{aligned}]t+{\\alpha }_{0}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0} & 6{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ 6{\\alpha }_{1}t & {\\alpha }_{0}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are constants to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The eigenvalues of $At$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=-6t,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=6t,\n$$\n\neach of multiplicity one. Thus, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}r(-6t)={e}^{-6t} \\\\ r(6t)={e}^{6t}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}-6t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{-6t} \\\\ 6t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{6t}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n- Subtracting the second equation from the first one, we get\n\n$$\n-12t{\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{-6t}-{e}^{6t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{12t}({e}^{-6t}-{e}^{6t}).\n$$\n\n- Adding the first and the second equations, we get\n\n$$\n2{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{-6t}+{e}^{6t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{0}=\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{-6t}+{e}^{6t}).\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0} & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{-6t}+{e}^{6t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{6t}+{e}^{-6t}) \\\\ 6{\\alpha }_{1}t & =6\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{12t}({e}^{-6t}-{e}^{6t}))t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{6t}-{e}^{-6t})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{6t}+{e}^{-6t} & {e}^{6t}-{e}^{-6t} \\\\ {e}^{6t}-{e}^{-6t} & {e}^{6t}+{e}^{-6t}\\end{aligned}].\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "a9bd7aaf-ed47-5adf-8e37-bc1f645e1959" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534128}}] :activity/title "Fundamental Matrices" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533125}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046645} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545985} :activity/id #uuid "f960835e-e823-56aa-a86d-984171474118" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050228 :topic/id #uuid "37d183d8-ce59-581c-9a89-fec7544aae03" :topic/math-academy-id 6376} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545937 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308611 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b642857-d5c7-5354-9582-e84b796c8d0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{3t}+{e}^{-3t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305568}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305568} :answer-field/id #uuid "191d5551-a712-5140-91a3-ad273a6ad519" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307600} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bfdf9ccd-6d68-52b8-90d2-de98adb98891" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{3t}+{e}^{-3t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306941}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306941} :answer-field/id #uuid "69784144-c83c-5567-a773-771397a8e2ee" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307859}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "ff10c64d-19c8-5825-958b-92dcd06d6e53" :question/math-academy-id "q-334387" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear ODEs ${x}^{′}=Ax$ with the matrix $A$ shown above. Compute the **standard** fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ for this system.\n\n| ${Φ}_{0}(t)=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | ${e}^{3t}-{e}^{-3t}$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | ${e}^{3t}-{e}^{-3t}$ | {{field-2}} |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $A$ is a constant $n\\times n$ matrix and $x(t)$ is an $n\\times 1$ column vector. The standard fundamental matrix of the linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ denoted ${Φ}_{0}(t),$ is the unique matrix solution of the initial value problem \n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}^{′}(t)=A{Φ}_{0}(t),\\,{Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere ${I}_{n}$ is the identity matrix. \n\nFurthermore, it can be shown that ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}.$\n\nWe have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{\\alpha }_{0} & 3{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ 3{\\alpha }_{1}t & {\\alpha }_{0}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are constants to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The eigenvalues of $At$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=-3t,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=3t,\n$$\n\nboth of multiplicity one. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}r(-3t) &={e}^{-3t} \\\\ r(3t) &={e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-3t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0} &={e}^{-3t} \\\\ 3t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0} &={e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n- Subtracting the second equation from the first one, we get\n\n$$\n-6t{\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{-3t}-{e}^{3t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{6t}({e}^{-3t}-{e}^{3t}).\n$$\n\n- Adding the first and the second equations, we get\n\n$$\n2{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{-3t}+{e}^{3t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{0}=\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{-3t}+{e}^{3t}).\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0} & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{-3t}+{e}^{3t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{3t}+{e}^{-3t}) \\\\ 3{\\alpha }_{1}t & =3\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{6t}({e}^{-3t}-{e}^{3t}))t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{3t}-{e}^{-3t})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{3t}+{e}^{-3t} & {e}^{3t}-{e}^{-3t} \\\\ {e}^{3t}-{e}^{-3t} & {e}^{3t}+{e}^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "64943c28-d0a3-53a2-88a5-d9adefa020e7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546015}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545970 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365731 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "af1db821-3c70-5204-8966-bfafe5646081" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\text{L2}$ and $\\text{L3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365733} {:answer/id #uuid "46a98a5b-5103-594c-8392-cbc3c0b9ab2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365734} {:answer/id #uuid "50a3b1bb-d180-5f10-92a0-b8485a60b0ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\text{L3}$ and $\\text{L4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365735} {:answer/id #uuid "0df6c99a-f4dc-500f-8d10-c44449ee81e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365736}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365733} :answer-field/id #uuid "3a13d5b5-5298-5a70-9317-9289780933f2" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365732} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ebbe2cee-547e-5c3f-a83e-3e7039b87b6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{L1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365738} {:answer/id #uuid "0688b4ed-31ff-50f2-b750-fc4b13f37d66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{L2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365739} {:answer/id #uuid "5ae6e5f8-c049-5a31-b2e3-f3115fead233" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{L3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365740} {:answer/id #uuid "90195316-825a-515d-b3c9-3a01074b1d1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{L5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365741}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365738} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d10fc4d-7319-57d5-84c9-f456089fcfdf" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365737} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "af6644a8-126d-525c-9a0e-dcbefee8db71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$Φ(t)$ is invertible for all $t$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365743} {:answer/id #uuid "dda2f86b-9e7f-5832-80e9-3a25481585f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${x}_{2}$ is a solution of the system" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365744} {:answer/id #uuid "014da003-c61d-5256-adef-8f54376054b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "${x}_{2}$ is differentiable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365745} {:answer/id #uuid "5dad3719-55ba-50da-94ff-2a2ef7836bfc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$A$ is diagonalizable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365746}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365744} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc5120a6-040d-570c-8bef-5b5b0b3b8898" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365742}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "9e4b9ffe-ff7e-5035-97f0-e9cfaaf038a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-336308" :question/problem "Consider the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant $2\\times 2$ matrix. Let $Φ(t)$ be a fundamental matrix for this system. A derivation showing that $Φ(t)$ satisfies the matrix differential equation ${Φ}^{′}(t)=A\\,Φ(t)$ is given below.\n\n*Hint: $[a,\\,b]$ denotes a matrix whose columns are vectors $a$ and $b.$*\n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ Let $Φ(t)=[{x}_{1}(t),\\,{x}_{2}(t)]$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ ${x}_{1}^{′}(t)=A{x}_{1}(t)$\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ ${x}_{2}^{′}(t)=A{x}_{2}(t)$\n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ ${Φ}^{′}(t)=[{x}_{1}^{′}(t),\\,{x}_{2}^{′}(t)]$\n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ ${Φ}^{′}(t)=[A{x}_{1}(t),\\,A{x}_{2}(t)]$\n\n$\\text{L6}:\\,$ ${Φ}^{′}(t)=A[{x}_{1}(t),\\,{x}_{2}(t)]$\n\n$\\text{L7}:\\,$ ${Φ}^{′}(t)=AΦ(t)$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L3}$ follows from the fact that {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L5}$ follows from line $\\text{L4}$ and substitution from lines {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L7}$ follows from line $\\text{L6}$ and substitution from line {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's justify each of the selected steps.\n\n- We first consider line $\\text{L3}.$ Since $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix, its columns ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are linearly independent solutions of the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t).$ This means that each column satisfies the differential equation. In particular, \n\n$$\n{x}_{2}^{′}(t)=A{x}_{2}(t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{L3}$ follows from the fact that ${x}_{2}\\text{ is a solution of the system}.$\n\n- Next, we consider lines $\\text{L4}$ and $\\text{L5}.$ By definition, the derivative of $Φ(t)$ is obtained by differentiating each column,\n\n$$\n{Φ}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\,| & | \\\\ {x}_{1}^{′}(t) & {x}_{2}^{′}(t) \\\\ \\,| & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nUsing ${x}_{1}^{′}(t)=A{x}_{1}(t)$ from $\\text{L2}$ and ${x}_{2}^{′}(t)=A{x}_{2}(t)$ from $\\text{L3},$ we substitute each derivative into the matrix to get\n\n$$\n{Φ}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\,| & | \\\\ A{x}_{1}(t) & A{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ \\,| & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThus, $\\text{L5}$ follows from $\\text{L4}$ by substitution from $\\text{L2}\\text{ and }\\text{L3}.$\n\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\text{L6}$ and $\\text{L7}.$ From $\\text{L6}$ we have\n\n$$\n{Φ}^{′}(t)=A[{x}_{1}(t),\\,{x}_{2}(t)].\n$$\n\nUsing the definition $Φ(t)=[{x}_{1}(t),\\,{x}_{2}(t)]$ from $\\text{L1},$ we substitute to obtain\n\n$$\n{Φ}^{′}(t)=AΦ(t).\n$$\n\nThus, $\\text{L7}$ follows from $\\text{L6}$ by substitution from $\\text{L1}.$"} :step/id #uuid "b2a1b6ac-99f1-5614-aa30-4a7585d5f1a5"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545983 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308609 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5d8223f6-d576-5e04-89fc-1464abef50dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}(5t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306050}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306050} :answer-field/id #uuid "0cae7130-92fe-567b-b98b-6f508a21ed99" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307569} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c3417787-0b37-54ba-b621-e6d55ac43b3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}(5t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306971}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306971} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c20d515-1f48-5aec-946c-716105bcbbf0" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307810} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dfed4f33-2d1f-51a1-a259-d0e2ac8c0f8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}(5t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307224}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186307224} :answer-field/id #uuid "e6a5f5a6-0b5f-5445-b33d-5f25bda05525" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308251} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "057dc0c1-9b91-5408-bfb2-87575fdd4609" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}(5t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305200}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305200} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8d91ade-2020-5dea-8c9b-3e7b3560fbbb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308313}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "64533ce4-1ae9-513d-ba89-484c2cf3a62c" :question/math-academy-id "q-334382" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 5 \\\\ -5 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear ODEs ${x}^{′}=Ax$ with the matrix $A$ shown above. Compute the **standard** fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ for this system.\n\n| ${Φ}_{0}(t)=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $A$ is a constant $n\\times n$ matrix and $x(t)$ is an $n\\times 1$ column vector. The standard fundamental matrix of the linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ denoted ${Φ}_{0}(t),$ is the unique matrix solution of the initial value problem \n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}^{′}(t)=A{Φ}_{0}(t),\\,{Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere ${I}_{n}$ is the identity matrix. \n\nFurthermore, it can be shown that ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}.$\n\nWe have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 5 \\\\ -5 & 0\\end{bmatrix}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{\\alpha }_{0} & 5{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ -5{\\alpha }_{1}t & {\\alpha }_{0}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are constants to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The eigenvalues of $At$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=5\\text{i}t,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-5\\text{i}t,\n$$\n\nwhich are complex conjugates. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r((5\\text{i})t) & ={e}^{(5\\text{i})t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}(5\\text{i})t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{0}+5\\text{i}{\\alpha }_{1}t & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we equate the real and imaginary parts, and solve for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0} &=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t) \\\\ 5{\\alpha }_{1}t &=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0} &=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1} &=\\frac{1}{5t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t) \\\\ 5{\\alpha }_{1}t & =5\\cdot \\frac{1}{5t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\cdot t \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "c739f31a-33e2-5b79-9d44-a58e2e368af7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545937}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545985 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221988 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4475a7f4-c95c-5c55-8109-0e9ea886a2a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545329}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545329} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa786690-2dc1-59f3-a44d-639db8237f3d" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220583} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10f335ea-9d53-5bd8-a277-0e71096d1701" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "e^{2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545230}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545230} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee77c4e2-6eb2-5db9-8618-4ba0d57d6223" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220584} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d4192bd6-a9b5-5e3c-a850-538acc11b68f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "e^{2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545587}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545587} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f7022e0-ee16-5d5b-b4f3-9eb2d8be983b" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220585} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "227c788b-a841-56fc-9c67-21ee4721d3f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "te^{2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545272}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545272} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c1b35ed-a68f-5e20-b0c8-7a1d98ca67ae" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220586}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d2008090-84f9-50fd-9af0-82b54be4b43f" :question/math-academy-id "q-333712" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear ODEs ${x}^{′}=Ax$ with the matrix $A$ shown above. Compute the **standard** fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ for this system.\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">${Φ}_{0}(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr></tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $A$ is a constant $n\\times n$ matrix and $x(t)$ is an $n\\times 1$ column vector. The standard fundamental matrix of the linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ denoted ${Φ}_{0}(t),$ is the unique matrix solution of the initial value problem \n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}^{′}(t)=A{Φ}_{0}(t),\\,{Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere ${I}_{n}$ is the identity matrix. \n\nFurthermore, it can be shown that ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}.$\n\nWe have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]t+{\\alpha }_{0}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & {\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ 0 & 2{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are constants to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The only eigenvalue of $At$ is \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=2t\n$$\n\nof multiplicity two. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}r(2t)={e}^{2t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\lambda }r(2t)={e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}2t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{2t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n- From the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{2t}.\n$$\n\n- Substituting this into the first one, we get\n\n$$\n2t{e}^{2t}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{2t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{2t}(1-2t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =2{e}^{2t}t+{e}^{2t}(1-2t) \\\\  & ={e}^{2t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}t & ={e}^{2t}t \\\\  & =t{e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t} & t{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}].\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "c89da063-d346-5af9-92b8-fb8eb176e13a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545983}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546015 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396895 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a5852157-70f8-5e93-89b0-59d8b774ff3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "integrating the given system" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396897} {:answer/id #uuid "990ecc42-c4e6-5578-a0da-d91b13097a44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "adding the vectors ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396898} {:answer/id #uuid "7722083e-e7dc-572c-9e33-46131eb0f8b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "multiplying $Φ(t)$ with it's inverse" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396899} {:answer/id #uuid "d7d2992c-33d6-5911-8f6b-9bda3e200baa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "the definition of a basis" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396900}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396900} :answer-field/id #uuid "b3682025-d096-56bd-bf59-0048dee77ced" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396896} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ea29c33a-1099-547c-826a-fd0e927691bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{Φ}^{-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396902} {:answer/id #uuid "eda2dd9e-6754-5d8c-bfd6-9ae76f866cf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{A}^{-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396903} {:answer/id #uuid "5c373e55-b761-5339-b7ae-18f49f46ac45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396904} {:answer/id #uuid "f31c325d-17cf-5219-a1cb-6658f11fd045" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Φ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396905}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396905} :answer-field/id #uuid "eedfdc9d-7a1b-5144-98ef-079b39f1a6cd" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396901} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42514761-6af9-5f0a-acd2-e9be42e06afe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "properties of invertible matrices" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396907} {:answer/id #uuid "7caef4bf-95b8-571b-aaa3-5210003d59f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "properties of diagonalizable matrices" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396908} {:answer/id #uuid "ca7d92b1-be8e-5617-880a-12beb90f314b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "definition of a fundamental matrix" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396909} {:answer/id #uuid "47b36cde-80ca-5b95-9fb8-d497515c1778" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "definition of matrix multiplication" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396910}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396909} :answer-field/id #uuid "25b2d3de-e14d-5e49-b765-fcc7eb25aafa" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396906} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "452a40cb-853e-5f67-a98a-fbeeccb65acb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "first row" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396912} {:answer/id #uuid "a4c30172-18f7-52e1-95bc-79d14f8a4bcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "diagonals" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396913} {:answer/id #uuid "c2045390-0f53-5f3d-8afa-92c13462bafa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "columns" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396914} {:answer/id #uuid "c0a29805-59cc-5557-a9b9-6313452c9c94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "rows" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396915}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396914} :answer-field/id #uuid "c98b9a18-7c6a-59cb-8ce5-cb6ffe8a471b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396911}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "accc1e17-f130-5583-b331-4d6a4ed72254" :question/math-academy-id "q-336106" :question/problem "Consider the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant $2\\times 2$ matrix. Let $Φ(t)$ be a fundamental matrix for this system. A derivation showing that every solution $x(t)$ can be written in the form $x(t)=Φ(t)\\,c$ for some constant vector $c=[{c}_{1},\\,{c}_{2}]^{T}$ is given below.\n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ Let ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ be the columns of $Φ(t)$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ form a basis of solutions\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ $x(t)$ can be written as $x(t)={c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t)$\n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ $Φ(t)\\,c={c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t)$\n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ $x(t)=Φ(t)\\,c$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L1}$ introduces the notation for the {{field-1}} of {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L2}$ follows from $\\text{L1}$ by the {{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L3}$ follows from line $\\text{L2}$ by {{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's justify each of the selected steps.\n\n- We first interpret line $\\text{L1}.$ Line $\\text{L1}$ introduces the notation for the $\\text{columns}$ of $Φ,$ namely the functions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$ These functions are the fundamental solutions of the system.\n\n- Next, we consider lines $\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L2}.$ Since $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix, its columns ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ form a basis of the solution space for our system. Thus, $\\text{L2}$ follows from $\\text{L1}$ by the $\\text{definition of a fundamental matrix}.$\n\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\text{L2}$ and $\\text{L3}.$ Once ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ form a basis, every solution $x(t)$ of the system can be expressed uniquely as a linear combination\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{L3}$ follows from $\\text{L2}$ by $\\text{the definition of a basis}.$"} :step/id #uuid "f58d9680-20f3-5d4a-86d9-25e71b4b53a0" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545970}}] :activity/title "Fundamental Matrices" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545199}]}
